     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-29 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>BCCI Considers Major WPL Expansion as Domestic Talent Pool Grows</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#WPL</span> <span>#BCCI</span> <span>#Women'        s Cricket</span></div>
       <p>The Board of Control for Cricket in India (BCCI) is actively considering a significant expansion of the Women's Premier League (WPL) from next year, with discussions underway to add one or two new franchises. This move aims to address the burgeoning pool of domestic talent in India, which currently struggles to find representation within the league's existing five-team structure.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-29/bcci-considers-major-wpl-expansion-as-domestic-talent-pool-g.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Devajit Saikia, the BCCI official overseeing the WPL, emphasized the necessity for growth, stating, &quot;Five teams are too few compared to the growing pool of women cricketers.&quot; His remarks come in the wake of the 2027 WPL auction held in Kochi on October 28, where approximately 150 cricketers vied for limited spots across the five established franchises. Only around 25 players secured contracts, leaving a substantial number of talented domestic players unaccommodated. The current five-team format has remained unchanged since the league's inception in 2023, prompting the BCCI to explore structural changes.</p><p>The proposed expansion, potentially coupled with increased player budgets for existing teams, is expected to create vital opportunities for more cricketers to gain exposure and competitive experience at the franchise level. This strategy is designed to ensure that a greater number of deserving players from India's domestic circuit receive a platform within the professional ecosystem. Saikia also highlighted the WPL's significant contribution to India's rise as a dominant force in women's cricket, citing the national team's ODI World Cup triumph last year as clear evidence of the league's positive impact on player development and international performance.</p><p>Further underlining the league's role in nurturing talent, Saikia lauded the selection of 15-year-old uncapped all-rounder Uma Kashvi, who was picked by Delhi Capitals for Rs 10 lakh during the auction. The 2027 WPL season is scheduled to run from January 14 to February 7. The BCCI's proactive approach signals a commitment to fostering the next generation of women cricketers and solidifying India's position in global women's cricket.</p><p>What to watch: Official announcement on new WPL teams and their home bases.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately reflects the BCCI's expansion plans and quotes provided in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.crictracker.com/cricket-news/five-teams-are-too-few-bcci-eyes-major-womens-premier-league-expansion-3198/" rel="noopener">Crictracker</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
