     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · BBC Business · 2026-10-29 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Jersey Minister Approves 2.8% Minimum Wage Increase</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#business</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Jersey's social security minister, Senator Elaine Millar, has accepted a recommendation to increase the minimum wage by 2.8%, raising the rate to £13.97 per hour effective 1 April 2027. This adjustment aligns with the June 2026 retail price index.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-29/jersey-minister-approves-28-minimum-wage-increase.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The minister also approved maintaining current trainee rates of £10.50 and £11.75 per hour for another year and accepted proportional increases for employee accommodation and meal allowances. Millar is currently reviewing potential changes to youth rates and formal trainee policies, noting that further public consultation may follow.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively captures the core details of the wage increase and the minister's stance within the target word count.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
