     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-29 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Maharashtra Village Produces 500 Litres of Drinking Water Daily From Air</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Maharashtra</span> <span>#water scarcity</span> <span>#technology</span> <span>#Meghdoot</span></div>
       <p>For years, residents of Wadji village in Maharashtra's Washim district faced a daily struggle, walking up to 1.5 km to fetch drinking water, a challenge amplified during scarce summer months. Wadji, home to nearly 2,000 people, is among communities in Vidarbha grappling with water scarcity, with 40 villages in Risod taluka categorized as semi-critical groundwater areas.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-29/maharashtra-village-produces-500-litres-of-drinking-water-da.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>A transformative solution has arrived in the form of Meghdoot, a machine that extracts moisture directly from the air and converts it into purified drinking water. This Atmospheric Water Generation technology, adopted by the Wadji Gram Panchayat, is reported to be the first and only instance of its kind in Maharashtra. The unit can produce up to 500 litres of water daily, offering a vital new source without relying on traditional groundwater or wells.</p><p>The Meghdoot system draws humid air, filters out particles, and uses a refrigeration mechanism to cool the air to its dew point, condensing water vapor. This collected water then undergoes purification, including activated carbon filtration and UV disinfection, to ensure it is suitable for consumption. Developed by CSIR-IICT scientists in Hyderabad in collaboration with Maithri Aquatech, the unit has been strategically installed at the Zilla Parishad Primary School and Anganwadi, making water readily accessible to children and local residents.</p><p>This innovation significantly reduces the arduous journey for water, bringing it closer to the community. While atmospheric water generation complements, rather than replaces, long-term solutions like groundwater conservation, its successful implementation in Wadji demonstrates a promising alternative for addressing water shortages in affected regions.</p><p>Why it matters: It offers a sustainable solution for water-scarce communities.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a thorough summary of the technology, its implementation, and the local context as described in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/innovation/maharashtra-village-wadji-water-from-air-technology-innovation-atmospheric-water-generation-india-12760747" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-10-29/maharashtra-village-produces-500-litres-of-drinking-water-da" data-title="Maharashtra Village Produces 500 Litres of Drinking Water Daily From Air"><b>Share:</b>
