     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-10-29 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New FASTag Local Pass Launches November 1: What It Means For Your Commute</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Personal Finance</span> <span>#FASTag</span> <span>#Toll Pass</span> <span>#Commuting</span></div>
       <p>A new FASTag concessional local pass, designed to ease the financial burden on everyday commuters, will launch on November 1. This pass is specifically for non-commercial vehicle owners who live within 20 kilometers of a National Highway fee plaza. Announced by the Ministry of Road Transport and Highways on October 27, it aims to make local travel more affordable, offering a distinct alternative to the existing FASTag Annual Pass.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-29/new-fastag-local-pass-launches-november-1-what-it-means-for-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The new local pass comes with an annual fee of either ₹1,500 or ₹3,000, depending on whether the toll plaza is located outside or within municipal or local town limits, respectively. It is crucial to understand that this pass is valid only for the specific fee plaza where it is issued and cannot be transferred to another location. Commuters can choose this new annual option or stick with the existing monthly pass for their selected plaza. However, this pass won't be available on highways with closed toll collection systems or where an alternative route or service road already exists. While it will be available at eligible toll plazas from November 1, you can also purchase it through the Rajmargyatra app starting December 1. Be aware that the government may revise these rates annually, with changes typically taking effect on April 1.</p><p>This new local pass differs significantly from the FASTag Annual Pass, which was launched on August 15, 2025, and currently costs ₹3,075, as per the Rajmargyatra website. While the Annual Pass allows cars, jeeps, and vans to cross multiple designated National Highway and National Expressway fee plazas without paying a user fee for each trip, the new local pass is strictly for a single, nearby plaza. If you regularly cross one specific toll plaza near your home, the local pass could be more economical. Conversely, if your travel frequently takes you across various National Highways and Expressways, the broader coverage of the FASTag Annual Pass might be more beneficial.</p><p>Before making a decision, it is advisable for all commuters to check the specific eligibility conditions and coverage details for their usual routes on the official NHAI or Rajmargyatra platforms. This will ensure you select the pass that best suits your travel patterns and maximizes your savings.</p><p>What to watch: Annual revisions to pass rates every April 1.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Well-structured article that clearly explains the new pass, its eligibility, and the comparison to the existing Annual Pass.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/new-fastag-local-pass-from-1-nov-costs-coverage-validity-rules-compared-with-annual-pass-which-one-should-you-buy-11793245719649.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
