     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-29 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Partisan 'Pink Slime' Sites Exploit Local News Trust</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Carnegie Mellon University researchers report that partisan media operations spent millions on Facebook ads to promote &quot;pink slime&quot; websites ahead of the 2020 and 2024 elections. These sites masquerade as legitimate local news to sway voters, often utilizing AI-generated content to increase credibility.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-29/partisan-pink-slime-sites-exploit-local-news-trust.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Campaigns specifically targeted swing states and demographics, with left-leaning sites focusing on women under 55 and right-leaning outlets targeting men over 55. By infiltrating local Facebook groups, these operations exploit existing community social trust. The study highlights how these low-quality, partisan sites fill the void left by declining traditional local journalism to influence public belief.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is concise, accurate, and captures the essential findings of the research study.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-stealth-partisan-outlets-exploit-local.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
